- Time: \(O(n^2)\) in the worst case, because we repeatedly search for the tail of flattened subtrees.
- Auxiliary space: \(O(h)\) for recursion.
This approach works, but repeatedly searching for a tail is unnecessary.

Approach 2 — Preorder Traversal + Store Nodes in a List
Idea
We know that the required linked-list order is preorder.

So we can first collect all nodes using preorder traversal:
Root → Left → Right


Then connect consecutive nodes:
nodes[0].right = nodes[1]
nodes[1].right = nodes[2]
nodes[2].right = nodes[3]
...


Set every node's left pointer to null.

This is straightforward, but it uses extra memory to store all node references.
Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public void flatten(TreeNode root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        List<TreeNode> nodes = new ArrayList<>();

        preorder(root, nodes);

        for (int i = 0; i < nodes.size(); i++) {

            TreeNode curr = nodes.get(i);

            curr.left = null;

            if (i + 1 < nodes.size()) {
                curr.right = nodes.get(i + 1);
            } else {
                curr.right = null;
            }
        }
    }

    private void preorder(
            TreeNode root,
            List<TreeNode> nodes) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        nodes.add(root);

        preorder(root.left, nodes);
        preorder(root.right, nodes);
    }
}


