- Auxiliary space: \(O(n)\) for the list, plus \(O(h)\) recursion space.
This approach has optimal time complexity but doesn't satisfy a constant-extra-space goal.

Approach 3 — Iterative Preorder Using a Stack
This approach also follows preorder, but instead of recursion or a node list, it uses a stack to control the traversal.
Idea
For each node popped from the stack:
1. Connect the previous node's right pointer to the current node.
2. Set the previous node's left pointer to null.
3. Push the current node's right child first.
4. Push the current node's left child second.
5. Set the current node as the previous node.
Why push right before left?

Because the stack is LIFO (last in, first out), so the left child will be processed first.
Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public void flatten(TreeNode root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        Stack<TreeNode> stack = new Stack<>();
        stack.push(root);

        TreeNode prev = null;

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {

            TreeNode curr = stack.pop();

            // Save children before rewiring pointers.
            if (curr.right != null) {
                stack.push(curr.right);
            }

            if (curr.left != null) {
                stack.push(curr.left);
            }

            // Connect previous node to current node.
            if (prev != null) {
                prev.left = null;
                prev.right = curr;
            }

            prev = curr;
        }

        // Ensure the final node terminates the list.
        prev.left = null;
        prev.right = null;
    }
}


