Approach 4 — Recursive Reverse Preorder (Your Code)
This is a very elegant solution. Rather than traversing in normal preorder, we process the tree in reverse preorder:
Right → Left → Root


A prev pointer remembers the node that should come immediately after the current node in the final flattened list.
Key idea
Suppose we process the nodes in this order:
6 → 5 → 4 → 3 → 2 → 1


When processing a node, we already know what its right pointer should reference:
root.right = prev;
root.left = null;
prev = root;


At node 6:
6.right = null
prev = 6


At node 5:
5.right = 6
prev = 5


At node 4:
4.right = 5
prev = 4


Continue until node 1.

This builds the final preorder chain from back to front.
Your code
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode() {}
 *     TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */
class Solution {
    TreeNode prev;

    public void flatten(TreeNode root) {
        if(root==null){
            prev=null;
            return;
        }
        solve(root);
        prev=null;
    }

    public void solve(TreeNode root){
        if(root==null){
            return;
        }
        solve(root.right);
        solve(root.left);
        root.right=prev;
        root.left=null;
        prev=root;
    }
}


Your code is correct. Resetting prev before beginning the traversal is also a clean defensive practice if you want to reuse the same Solution instance:
class Solution {

    TreeNode prev;

    public void flatten(TreeNode root) {
        prev = null;
        solve(root);
    }

    private void solve(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        solve(root.right);
        solve(root.left);

        root.right = prev;
        root.left = null;
        prev = root;
    }
}


The second version has the same algorithm and makes the initialization explicit.

