Although the inner loop searches for a predecessor, the right-edge traversals across these searches are amortized linear overall; the algorithm doesn't repeatedly traverse every subtree.

This is the best approach if the interviewer specifically asks for an in-place solution with constant extra space.

Comparison of All Approaches
Approach	Main technique	Time	Auxiliary space
1. Recursive tail search	Flatten subtrees, find tail, splice	\(O(n^2)\) worst	\(O(h)\)
2. Preorder + list	Store nodes and reconnect	\(O(n)\)	\(O(n)\)
3. Iterative stack	Generate preorder and link previous node	\(O(n)\)	\(O(n)\)
4. Reverse preorder — your code	Right → Left → Root, using prev	\(O(n)\)	\(O(h)\)
5. Morris-style in-place	Predecessor rewiring	\(O(n)\)	\(O(1)\)

Here:
- \(n\) = number of nodes.
- \(h\) = height of the tree.
Which approach should you remember?
For normal interview preparation, I recommend these two:

Recursive reverse preorder: easiest elegant recursive solution.
solve(root.right);
solve(root.left);

root.right = prev;
root.left = null;
prev = root;


Morris-style in-place: use when constant auxiliary space is important.
if (curr.left != null) {
    TreeNode predecessor = curr.left;

    while (predecessor.right != null) {
        predecessor = predecessor.right;
    }

    predecessor.right = curr.right;
    curr.right = curr.left;
    curr.left = null;
}

curr = curr.right;



